    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 751,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 581,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116368,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 581,
        "headingText": "<p >Lymphoepithelial cysts affect multiple locations, including the parotid, lateral neck, and oral cavity, particularly the floor of the mouth {13608417; 28936296}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-11-21T12:54:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 752,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 582,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116369,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 582,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In immunocompetent individuals, lymphoepithelial cysts present as a unilateral painless mass. Patients with HIV infection may develop bilateral disease {3393663; 31456841}.</p>\r\n<h5>Imaging</h5>\r\n<p>Imaging is useful for confirming their simple cystic nature and delineating their anatomical location. Lymphoepithelial cysts appear as well-circumscribed T2-hyperintense and T1-weighted low-signal lesions on MRI, which may demonstrate thin peripheral postcontrast enhancement. On ultrasound, the lesions are hypoechoic/anechoic with well-defined margins. In HIV-related cases, enlarged adenoidal tissue and cervical lymph nodes are also often noted.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:31:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 753,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 583,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116370,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 583,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lymphoepithelial cysts arise over a broad age range, with a peak in the fourth to fifth decade of life {7535539; 9496829; 20303054}. They have classically been described in AIDS and are reported to occur in as many as 6% and 10% of HIV-positive adults and children, respectively {1407984; 3393663}. Cystic lesions with a similar morphology also occur in middle-aged immunocompetent individuals and patients with Sj&ouml;gren syndrome {10335942; 31456841}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:31:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 754,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 584,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116371,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 584,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology is unclear. It has been postulated that lymphoepithelial cysts arise from epithelial inclusions located in intraparotid and/or periparotid lymph nodes that subsequently undergo cystic dilatation due to obstruction {3393663; 6809660}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:31:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 755,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 585,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116372,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 585,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:31:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 762,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 592,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116373,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 592,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Lesions may be unilocular or multilocular, with a mean size of 25&nbsp;mm {29491615}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:31:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 756,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 586,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116374,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 586,
        "headingText": "<p>Lymphoepithelial cyst is a dilated cystic structure lined by squamous epithelium with variable degrees of maturation and/or columnar epithelium. The epithelium lacks cytomorphological evidence of malignancy. The cyst wall exhibits a dense lymphoid infiltrate with well-developed lymphoid follicles, and the epithelium is commonly permeated by lymphocytes. In the setting of AIDS and Sj&ouml;gren syndrome, the background parenchyma may show features of lymphoepithelial sialadenitis. In HIV-positive patients, immunoreactivity for the major HIV-1 core protein p24 can be seen in follicular dendritic cells and interfollicular multinucleated giant cells within the lymphoid stroma adjacent to the cyst {8394048; 28963391}.</p>\r\n<h5>Differential diagnosis</h5>\r\n<p>Branchial cleft cysts and isolated lymphoepithelial cysts have striking overlapping morphology. Because both lesions have a benign clinical course, the distinction between them has no practical implications. Cystic salivary gland neoplasms with tumour-associated lymphoid proliferation can mimic a lymphoepithelial cyst.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-01-18T15:35:27"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 757,
    "bookid": 52,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 587,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 116375,
        "bookId": 52,
        "chapterId": 342,
        "headingId": 587,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Smears show a mixed lymphoid population and rare benign epithelial elements in a background of proteinaceous fluid {2294703; 10450101; 12357495}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-10-02T13:31:31"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
